</head>
<body>
<div class="report">

  <!-- Header -->
  <div class="header">
    <div class="ticker">NYSE: AXP</div>
    <h1>American Express Historical Stock Performance</h1>
    <div class="subtitle">Comprehensive Investor Analysis: 1972–2026</div>
    <div class="date-range">{first_date.strftime('%B %d, %Y')} — {last_date.strftime('%B %d, %Y')} &nbsp;|&nbsp; {int(years_span)}+ Years of Data &nbsp;|&nbsp; {len(df):,} Trading Days</div>
  </div>

  <!-- KPI Dashboard -->
  <div class="kpi-grid">
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="kpi-label">Cumulative Total Return</div>
      <div class="kpi-value positive">{cumulative_return:,.0f}%</div>
      <div class="kpi-sub">${first_close:.2f} → ${last_close:.2f}</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="kpi-label">Compounded Annual Growth Rate</div>
      <div class="kpi-value positive">{cagr:.2f}%</div>
      <div class="kpi-sub">{years_span:.1f}-year annualized</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="kpi-label">Maximum Drawdown</div>
      <div class="kpi-value negative">{max_dd:.1f}%</div>
      <div class="kpi-sub">{max_dd_peak_date.strftime('%Y-%m-%d')} → {max_dd_date.strftime('%Y-%m-%d')}</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="kpi-label">Annualized Volatility</div>
      <div class="kpi-value accent">{annualized_vol:.1f}%</div>
      <div class="kpi-sub">Daily σ × √252</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card">
      <div class="kpi-label">Total Dividends Paid/Share</div>
      <div class="kpi-value accent">${total_dividends:.2f}</div>
      <div class="kpi-sub">{len(div_events)} distributions</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Section 1: Executive Overview -->
  <div class="section">
    <div class="section-title">Executive Overview</div>
    <div class="section-subtitle">Summary of AXP's 50+ year stock trajectory and key takeaways</div>

    <div class="narrative">
      American Express (AXP) has delivered a <strong>cumulative return of {cumulative_return:,.0f}%</strong> over {years_span:.0f} years,
      translating to a <strong>CAGR of {cagr:.2f}%</strong>. A $10,000 investment at the start of 1972 would be worth approximately
      <strong>${last_close / first_close * 10000:,.0f}</strong> by March 2026 (price appreciation only, excluding dividends).
      The stock reached an all-time high of <strong>${ath_price:.2f}</strong> on {ath_date.strftime('%B %d, %Y')},
      and its lowest adjusted close was <strong>${atl_price:.2f}</strong> on {atl_date.strftime('%B %d, %Y')}.
      The worst peak-to-trough decline was <strong>{abs(max_dd):.1f}%</strong>, occurring during the {max_dd_peak_date.strftime('%Y')}–{max_dd_date.strftime('%Y')} period.
      Including <strong>${total_dividends:.2f}</strong> in cumulative dividends per share across {len(div_events)} payments, AXP has served
      as both a growth and income holding over the long term.
    </div>

    <div class="two-col">
      <div class="callout">
        <div class="callout-label">All-Time High</div>
        <div style="font-size:1.5rem;font-weight:700;font-family:var(--mono)">${ath_price:.2f}</div>
        <div style="color:var(--text2);font-size:0.85rem">{ath_date.strftime('%B %d, %Y')}</div>
      </div>
      <div class="callout">
        <div class="callout-label">All-Time Low (Adjusted)</div>
        <div style="font-size:1.5rem;font-weight:700;font-family:var(--mono)">${atl_price:.2f}</div>
        <div style="color:var(--text2);font-size:0.85rem">{atl_date.strftime('%B %d, %Y')}</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Section 2: Long-Term Price Trend Analysis -->
  <div class="section">
    <div class="section-title">Long-Term Price Trend Analysis</div>
    <div class="section-subtitle">Multi-decade price trajectory, bull/bear phases, and CAGR across key sub-periods</div>

    <div class="chart-container">
      <div class="chart-title">Adjusted Closing Price — Monthly (1972–2026)</div>
      <canvas id="priceChart" height="320"></canvas>
    </div>

    <div class="narrative">
      AXP's price history reveals several distinct regimes. After a period of stagnation through the mid-1980s
      (adjusted prices hovered near $1–$3), the stock entered a sustained uptrend in the late 1980s and 1990s,
      peaking above $25 (adjusted) before the dot-com correction. The 2008 financial crisis inflicted the
      deepest drawdown on record, with the stock losing {abs(max_dd):.1f}% from peak to trough. The subsequent
      recovery and the 2010–2019 bull market drove prices to new highs, a trajectory interrupted only briefly
      by the COVID-19 crash of early 2020.
    </div>

    <div class="table-container">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Sub-Period</th><th>Start Price</th><th>End Price</th><th>Total Return</th><th>CAGR</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>{sub_cagr_rows}</tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <div class="chart-container">
      <div class="chart-title">CAGR by Sub-Period</div>
      <canvas id="cagrChart" height="260"></canvas>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Section 3: Volatility & Risk Assessment -->
  <div class="section">
    <div class="section-title">Volatility & Risk Assessment</div>
    <div class="section-subtitle">Drawdown analysis, volatility regimes, and worst-case loss episodes</div>

    <div class="chart-container">
      <div class="chart-title">Underwater Chart — Drawdown from Peak (%)</div>
      <canvas id="drawdownChart" height="280"></canvas>
    </div>

    <div class="narrative">
      The underwater chart above shows every period where AXP traded below its prior all-time high.
      The most severe episode was the <strong>2008 Global Financial Crisis</strong>, which sent AXP into a
      <strong>{abs(major_episodes[0]['drawdown_pct'] if major_episodes else max_dd):.1f}%</strong> drawdown —
      reflecting the company's direct exposure to credit markets and consumer spending contraction.
      The <strong>COVID-19 crash</strong> of March 2020 produced a sharp but shorter-lived drawdown,
      with recovery occurring within months as fiscal stimulus and vaccine optimism restored investor confidence.
    </div>

    <div class="table-container">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Peak Date</th><th>Peak Price</th><th>Trough Date</th><th>Trough Price</th><th>Drawdown</th><th>Decline Duration</th><th>Recovery Date</th><th>Recovery Duration</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>{dd_episode_rows}</tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <div class="two-col">
      <div class="callout">
        <div class="callout-label">Worst Single-Day Return</div>
        <div class="kpi-value negative">{df['Daily_Return'].dropna().min()*100:.2f}%</div>
        <div style="color:var(--text2);font-size:0.85rem">{df.loc[df['Daily_Return'].idxmin(), 'Date'].strftime('%B %d, %Y')}</div>
      </div>
      <div class="callout">
        <div class="callout-label">Best Single-Day Return</div>
        <div class="kpi-value positive">{df['Daily_Return'].dropna().max()*100:.2f}%</div>
        <div style="color:var(--text2);font-size:0.85rem">{df.loc[df['Daily_Return'].idxmax(), 'Date'].strftime('%B %d, %Y')}</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Section 4: Trading Volume Analysis -->
  <div class="section">
    <div class="section-title">Trading Volume Analysis</div>
    <div class="section-subtitle">Liquidity trends, structural shifts, and volume-price correlation</div>

    <div class="chart-container">
      <div class="chart-title">Average Monthly Trading Volume</div>
      <canvas id="volumeChart" height="280"></canvas>
    </div>

    <div class="narrative">
      Volume data is unavailable before the mid-1980s (shown as zero in the dataset). From the mid-1980s onward,
      average daily volume has ranged from roughly 1–5 million shares, with notable spikes during the 2008 crisis
      and the 2020 COVID crash. A structural increase in average volume is visible from the 1990s into the 2000s,
      followed by a gradual decline in the 2010s and 2020s as algorithmic trading reduced visible share turnover.
      Volume spikes are consistently associated with high-volatility price events.
    </div>

    <div class="table-container">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Date</th><th>Close Price</th><th>Volume</th><th>Ratio vs. 50-day Avg</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>{vol_spike_rows}</tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Section 5: Dividend History & Stock Splits -->
  <div class="section">
    <div class="section-title">Dividend History & Stock Splits</div>
    <div class="section-subtitle">Income generation profile and corporate actions over five decades</div>

    <div class="chart-container">
      <div class="chart-title">Annual Dividend Yield (%)</div>
      <canvas id="divYieldChart" height="280"></canvas>
    </div>

    <div class="narrative">
      AXP has paid <strong>{len(div_events)}</strong> dividend distributions over the dataset period, totaling
      <strong>${total_dividends:.2f}</strong> per share on a split-adjusted basis. The dividend yield has varied
      significantly over time, tending to rise during price declines (as yield is inversely related to price)
      and compress during bull markets. AXP suspended or reduced dividends during the 2008 crisis period,
      reflecting capital preservation priorities. The company has consistently restored and grown its dividend
      in subsequent years, underscoring its commitment to returning capital to shareholders.
    </div>

    <div class="two-col">
      <div class="table-container">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Date</th><th>Dividend</th><th>Close</th><th>Yield</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>{recent_div_rows}</tbody>
        </table>
        <div style="padding:10px 14px;font-size:0.78rem;color:var(--text2)">Top 10 largest dividend payments by amount</div>
      </div>
      <div class="table-container">
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Date</th><th>Split Ratio</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>{split_rows}</tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Section 6: Decade-by-Decade Comparison -->
  <div class="section">
    <div class="section-title">Decade-by-Decade Performance Comparison</div>
    <div class="section-subtitle">Consistent metrics across six decades for direct comparison</div>

    <div class="table-container">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Decade</th><th>Start Price</th><th>End Price</th><th>Total Return</th><th>CAGR</th><th>Ann. Volatility</th><th>Max Drawdown</th><th>Total Dividends</th><th>Div Payments</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>{decade_table_rows}</tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <div class="chart-container">
      <div class="chart-title">Decade Comparison — CAGR, Volatility & Max Drawdown</div>
      <canvas id="decadeChart" height="300"></canvas>
    </div>

    <div class="narrative">
      The decade-by-decade comparison reveals that AXP's strongest growth occurred in the <strong>1980s and 1990s</strong>,
      driven by financial sector deregulation and the expansion of consumer credit.
      The <strong>2000s</strong> were the weakest decade, bookended by the dot-com bust and the 2008 financial crisis,
      delivering negative total returns. The <strong>2010s</strong> saw a robust recovery as AXP repositioned its business
      and benefited from the longest bull market in U.S. history. The <strong>2020s</strong> (partial) have continued
      the uptrend despite the COVID-19 interruption, with volatility elevated relative to the 2010s but
      returns remaining positive.
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- Footer -->
  <div class="footer">
    <p>Data source: American Express (AXP) Stock Data 1972–2026 (Kaggle: anadiskt/american-express-axp-stock-data-19722026)</p>
    <p>All prices are split/dividend-adjusted. Report generated {datetime.now().strftime('%Y-%m-%d %H:%M UTC')}.</p>
    <p style="margin-top:8px;font-size:0.75rem;color:var(--text2)">This report is for informational purposes only and does not constitute investment advice.</p>
  </div>

</div>

<!-- Chart.js Scripts -->
<script>
Chart.defaults.color = '#9ca0b0';
Chart.defaults.borderColor = '#2e3145';
Chart.defaults.font.family = "'Segoe UI', system-ui, sans-serif";
Chart.defaults.font.size = 12;

// 1. Price Chart
new Chart(document.getElementById('priceChart'), {{
  type: 'line',
  data: {{
    labels: {json.dumps(price_dates)},
    datasets: [{{
      label: 'Close Price (Adjusted)',
      data: {json.dumps(price_values)},
      borderColor: '#6366f1',
      backgroundColor: 'rgba(99,102,241,0.08)',
